    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "which with $n = 2$ (a diallelic locus) reduces further to Equation 4.12a. Thus, under random mating, $\\sigma_A^2$ for a locus is simply equal to the mean-squared additive effect, multiplied by two to account for diploidy. More generally, since $E[\\alpha] = 0$, $\\sigma_A^2 = E[(\\alpha_i + \\alpha_j)^2]$.",
      "source_page": 93,
      "printed_page": 73,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter4:p027:b12"
      ],
      "bbox": [
