    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "In general, inbreeding inflates the additive genetic variance by causing correlations among the effects of alleles within the same individuals. However, because the additive effect itself is a function of f, inbreeding does not necessarily simply increase the additive genetic variance by the factor $ (1 + f) $. From Kempthorne (1957),",
      "source_page": 93,
      "printed_page": 73,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter4:p027:b16"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        36.0,
