      "content_sha256": "c2f55a9c9392cd5c9c13995ff30adce8af2de55eb29fa43a10d9c9500deeffe1"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "2a, $ \\alpha_{i} = \\alpha_{ir} = a $, and the additive genetic variance in an inbred population is, in fact, $ (1 + f) $ times greater than that under random mating. However, with any level of dominance, $ \\alpha_{i} \\neq \\alpha_{ir} $ under inbreeding, and the change in additive genetic variance with f is not likely to be linear.",
      "source_page": 94,
      "printed_page": 74,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter4:p028:b2"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        130.0,
